From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add LED torch module and nyx-test-led

NYX_DEVICE_LED has been declared in nyx-lib since the beginning, with
nyx_led_set_brightness()/get_brightness(), and no module has ever implemented
it - on either the native or the hybris side. So LuneOS has never had a torch at
any layer. The one trace of one is a "Flashlight" entry in cardshell's system
menu calling luna://ca.canucksoftware.systoolsmgr/flashOn, a webOS 1.x homebrew
service that was never part of any LuneOS image and so reports "Off" forever.

This implements the two backends that do not need libhybris. Brightness is a
percentage, per nyx_led.h.

MediaTek is probed first and looks nothing like the others: CONFIG_MTK_FLASHLIGHT
(mindphone) puts the torch behind /dev/flashlight ioctls, and that device's
/sys/class/leds holds only keyboard-backlight, lcd-backlight and vibrator -
checked on hardware - so there is no LED-class node for the other path to find.

Three things about the MTK interface that only the hardware revealed:

- The driver also exposes flashlight_strobe under /sys/class/flashlight_core,
  and it is useless here: flashlights_led_gpio.c implements strobe_store as
  enable, msleep(dur), disable - a one-shot flash capped at 3000 ms. A torch has
  to come from the ioctl path, where SET_TIME_OUT_TIME_MS(0) disarms the driver's
  auto-off hrtimer and SET_ONOFF(1) then stays on.

- The ioctl takes 1-based *ids*, which flashlight-core converts to the 0-based
  *indices* its device table is keyed on ("return type_id - 1"), while the sysfs
  strobe attribute takes indices directly. So "0 0 0 1 500" is right for sysfs
  and wrong for the ioctl: id 0 fails validation before the device lookup and
  returns EINVAL, which is indistinguishable from "no driver registered".

- Opening /dev/flashlight is not proof of a torch. flashlight-core creates the
  node at its own init whether or not a sub-driver registered, so the module
  asks GET_PROTOCOL_VERSION first - resolved after the device lookup, so it fails
  exactly when nothing registered - and falls through to the LED class if it
  fails, rather than claiming a backend that can never work.

Duty is capped at the torch end of the range. led_gpio_enable() picks its pin
state from the duty - <= 2 is the torch pin, above that the flash pin - and flash
current is specified for a strobe of milliseconds. Since holding the light on
indefinitely is precisely this module's job, exposing the upper range would let a
caller cook the LED by asking for 100%. Percentages map onto 0..2 only.

The ioctl numbers and struct flashlight_user_arg are restated from
drivers/misc/mediatek/flashlight/flashlight.h because there is no uapi export.
The _IOR on commands that plainly write is the kernel's own quirk, kept verbatim:
the direction bits are part of the command number, so "fixing" them would produce
an ioctl the driver does not answer to.

Two sysfs shapes are handled as well, for the rest of our devices:

  plain LED class    writing <node>/brightness lights the torch. What
                     LEDS_CLASS_FLASH devices such as tissot and rosy present:
                     `brightness` is the torch current, while flash_brightness
                     and flash_strobe drive the momentary flash, untouched here.

  current + switch   qcom's qpnp-flash-v2, as on sargo. led:torch_0/brightness
                     only arms a current; nothing lights until
                     led:switch_0/brightness goes to 1. The switch node is
                     derived from the torch node's name, since the driver names
                     them as a pair.

Ordering in that second shape is deliberate: arm the current before enabling the
switch, cut the switch before disarming. The reverse leaves the hardware in a
state - switch on, current zero - that is meaningless and that a failed write
would strand it in.

Paths come from [module.led] torch_path / torch_switch_path in /etc/nyx.conf when
set, otherwise derived by scanning /sys/class/leds. "torch" is preferred over
"flash": where a device exposes both, the flash node is the momentary strobe.
The shipped nyx.conf deliberately does not declare these keys - detection covers
what we have, and luneos-device-config's generator warns about undeclared keys -
so they exist purely as a per-device escape hatch.

Owned per machine by NYXMOD_OW_LEDTORCH, like the LED controller: TRUE (the
default) builds this module, FALSE hands the torch to nyx-modules-hybris, which
drives the Android camera service through droid_media_camera_set_torch_mode() -
the route that works where /sys/class/leds belongs to the Android side, and the
one SailfishOS uses.

nyx-test-led is the matching CLI - on/off/set/get - kept separate from
nyx-test-ledcontroller because they drive genuinely different nyx devices. It
takes -t <seconds> to hold the torch on: closing the nyx device switches the
torch off, deliberately, so a crashed service cannot leave it burning, which
means a one-shot "on" lights it only for as long as the process takes to exit.
A service holds the handle instead; -t is the equivalent for a CLI, and without
it the tool appears to do nothing at all.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS specific]
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
diff -urN nm3.base/src/CMakeLists.txt nm3/src/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/src/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-30 21:14:20.527237148 +0200
+++ b/src/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-30 21:33:17.953093742 +0200
@@ -123,3 +123,20 @@
 # built by the other recipe entirely, which is exactly where a way to poke the
 # notification LED is most wanted.
 add_subdirectory(led_controller_test)
+
+# The torch has two backends, so like the LED controller it needs a per-machine
+# owner. Here it is the sysfs one; NYXMOD_OW_LEDTORCH FALSE hands it to
+# nyx-modules-hybris, which drives the Android camera service instead - the
+# route that works where /sys/class/leds belongs to the Android side.
+#
+# Defaulted rather than required of all 57 machine files: sysfs is the right
+# answer for every native device, and a halium machine that needs the other
+# backend says so explicitly.
+if(NOT DEFINED NYXMOD_OW_LEDTORCH)
+    set(NYXMOD_OW_LEDTORCH TRUE)
+endif()
+
+if(NYXMOD_OW_LEDTORCH)
+    add_subdirectory(led_torch)
+endif()
+add_subdirectory(led_test)
